                        StudyTopic(id: "security_implications_obj3_q10", number: 185, question: "When validating a complex sharing model post-migration, which combination of tools (Record Access page, SOQL on share objects, user login simulation) provides the most comprehensive verification?", answer: "Use all three in combination:\n\n- SOQL on Share objects: enumerate all sharing rows for sampled records to confirm expected sources and access levels are present.\n\n- Record Access page (Sharing button): spot-check individual records for a sample of representative users to confirm the correct sharing sources appear.\n\n- Login As simulation: log in as representative users across each role/profile combination and navigate to records that should and should not be visible, confirming access boundaries.\n\nAdd Sharing Debugger for any discrepancies found during the above steps."),
                        StudyTopic(id: "security_implications_obj3_q11", number: 186, question: "How the Salesforce Event Monitoring logs (Login Event, Report Export) can be used to audit whether users accessed records they should not have been able to see.", answer: "Event Monitoring (Shield add-on) provides log files for events like:\n\n- ReportEvent: captures when a user ran a report and which records were returned.\n\n- ListViewEvent: logs list view access.\n\n- ApiEvent: logs API queries and returned record counts.\n\n- LoginEvent: tracks login time, IP, and method.\n\nQuery `EventLogFile` via SOQL or download logs, then cross-reference accessed records against the user's expected sharing access. Discrepancies indicate either a sharing misconfiguration or a permission override (View All Data, etc.) that needs review.")
